' ============================================================================
' pseudostate-hiding.puml — a pseudostate ([*] initial/final, and the same
' goes for <<choice>>, <<fork>>, <<join>>, <<entryPoint>>/<<exitPoint>>,
' history) is not itself an entity you can tag when written with its
' shorthand syntax ([*], or a bare stereotype with no name). Hiding/removing
' the state at the other end of its (typically only) connector hides that
' link, via the normal state-hides-its-own-links cascade — but the
' pseudostate glyph itself was never tagged, so it stays fully visible,
' now with nothing attached to it.
'
' This bites pseudostates much harder than ordinary states: an ordinary
' state usually has several neighbours, so losing one is often unremarkable,
' but an initial or final pseudostate typically has exactly one connector,
' so losing it always leaves a dangling, unconnected glyph.
'
' The fix: give the pseudostate an explicit name and stereotype instead of
' the shorthand, and tag it identically to whatever it connects to. See
' broken vs fixed below — rendered as pseudostate-hiding-broken.svg/.png and
' pseudostate-hiding-fixed.svg/.png.
' ============================================================================

@startuml pseudostate-hiding-broken

hide empty description

state A $tag $scenario_state
state B

' Shorthand [*]: cannot be tagged, so it can't be hidden along with A.
[*] --> A
A --> B

hide $scenario_state

@enduml

@startuml pseudostate-hiding-fixed

hide empty description

state A $tag $scenario_state
state B

' Explicit, named, tagged initial pseudostate: hides along with A, because
' it now carries the same tag rather than relying on cascade from the link.
' Tags immediately before the stereotype, no space, as always.
state SI $tag $scenario_state<<start>>
SI --> A
A --> B

hide $scenario_state

@enduml
